module gunbc.plans.desired_state_follows_the_machine

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.markdown_helpers { h2, h3, p, li, ul, quote }

// THE CARRIER: the operator's tiered breakage report of 2026-11-21, read once, in full, and turned into
// one intention. WHAT THIS CARRIER IS NOT: it is not a host runbook and it is not a phase list. Every
// specimen named below was found by COLLISION -- a lane paid for it while doing other work -- so the
// intention is about who reads the machine, not about fixing the four hosts the report happened to
// touch. Per gunbc.design_argument::plans_are_intentions this carries what, why and a done-when; the
// realization lives in the work items the operator opens, each of which names this plan.

fn desired_state_follows_the_machine_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    quote([
      p("**Goal:** what the repository says about a host is matched by what that host is, and a divergence refuses at the moment a roster would rely on it -- not later, as a slow request, a refused connection, or an empty column in a receipt."),
      p("**Provenance:** the operator's tiered breakage report, relayed 2026-11-21 (tiers 0 through 5, plus the operator's own recursion: tiers 1-3 are one thing on three axes). One item of that report is recorded here as a mis-stated premise rather than a defect; see WHAT THIS PLAN DOES NOT BUILD ON."),
    ]),
    h2("What"),
    p("The model names what each host runs, and nothing reads the machine. Every per-host claim -- which units are enabled, which hardware enumerates, how the host is reached and keyed, what cap actually binds a role, what backing a presupposed limit rests on -- is written from an intention or an operator relay and then stands unread. Desired state and the machine therefore drift on three axes, and each drift is discovered only by colliding with it:"),
    ul([
      li("**Units against hardware.** A unit stays enabled after the hardware it depends on stops enumerating. butch (operator_host_srv14): all four NVIDIA A2 boards left the machine on 2026-11-17 -- the tree knows this, and knows the PCI read returns no NVIDIA function -- yet strata-pool@a and strata-pool@b remain enabled and would start at boot. dan (operator_host_6ce85c, the host the report calls h11): strata-expert-lock.service is enabled, pinning GiB for A16s that are gone, every boot, serving nothing."),
      li("**Address against identity.** A host is reached by an address, and the address is not the host. butch moved 192.168.1.205 -> 192.168.1.238 in nine days and moved NIC (enP2p4s0 MAC ..ca:9c -> enP3p3s0f1 MAC ..ca:9b); the address it landed on carried a previous occupant's host key, so the failure was host-key-verification-failed -- the same symptom as an attack, resolved by matching an ed25519 fingerprint by hand. srv3 was unreachable at its former address, and the address rows concede their own instability (butch's LAN endpoint is labeled a DHCP lease observation, not intent, and srv4's host and BMC addresses 'remain only as stable as their leases')."),
      li("**Caps against working set.** A limit is set in one place and its violation surfaces somewhere else, as degradation rather than refusal. srv4's serving engines are bounded by a service-level MemoryHigh of 40 GiB, which put the expert set below residency: 5.1 tok/s with 41.5 GB read per request, against 24.5-29.1 tok/s at 56 GiB -- a fivefold cliff with no alarm anywhere. srv4 answered HTTP 429 at concurrency 4, and the 429 was found by colliding with it. The same shape one level down: Strata's prefix cache defaults to six checkpoints, four concurrent agents evict one another through it (three hits against fifty misses), and every turn pays a 32 s re-prefill where a hit costs 3 s."),
    ]),
    p("This plan owns ONE thing those axes share: the read. A standing producer that, per host, enumerates hardware, enabled units, host-key identity, the caps that actually bind each role, and the backing each presupposed limit rests on; compares each against what the model claims; and refuses, typed per axis, where they diverge. It owns the rule that a host is admitted to a roster only through that comparison. It owns no mutation: the read says, converge does."),
    h2("Why"),
    p("Because every one of the specimens above was paid for by a lane that was trying to do something else, and the paid-for part is the discovery, not the fix. The tree already contains each half of the fix -- the hardware population (gunbc.fleet.fleet_physical_inventory, gunbc.fleet.gpu_device_observation), the unit reads (gunbc.systemctl_show_read, whose three arms already refuse rather than report empty, and the host_unit_retirement mode of fleet-converge proposed in PR 16831), the identity route (gunbc.fleet.fleet_tailnet, gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment, extdeps.ssh.known_hosts), the cap and backing route (gunbc.host.memory_envelope, gunbc.host.host_axis_caps, gunbc.host.host_swap_backing, gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory) -- and no consumer joins those halves to the machine on a schedule. That is exactly the standing gap gunbc.design_argument::consumption names: the halves exist, the join does not, so each axis keeps producing new specimens at new hosts."),
    p("The three axes are also one failure of standing: an unread claim about a machine is consumed as if it were a reading. That is why this is one intention and not three -- the report's own recursion -- and why the done-when is written as one consumer refusing, not as four hosts repaired."),
    h2("What this plan does NOT own (reconciliation, not a fork)"),
    ul([
      li("**Tier 1 (units vs hardware) is an instance, not a new subject.** gunbc.plans.fleet_hardware_fact_consumption already owns the principle that changing installed hardware changes the configuration, admission and capacity it bears on, and already carries butch's departed A2s. It stays the owner of the hardware-to-derivation join. This plan adds the run-time half -- the unit enabled against hardware that no longer enumerates -- and cites that plan rather than re-deriving it."),
      li("**Tier 2 (address vs identity) is already owned.** gunbc.plans.fleet_machine_authority owns machine identity and reach, the ruling of 2026-11-17 that fleet hosts are reached by tailnet name, and the enrollment route in gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment. This plan does not create an identity subject; it asks that the identity claim be *read back* -- the enrolled fingerprint, not the address -- as one of the five comparisons."),
      li("**Tier 3 (caps) is already owned at the slot.** gunbc.host.memory_envelope judges a slot's declared shape, and the recurring row gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot already states its next trigger as a scheduled live read of every fleet slot refusing on drift. This plan is where that trigger is grounded for *serving* caps too, and it does not touch the envelope's judgment."),
      li("**The untagged join (report 0.2) is already owned.** gunbc.tailscale_join_converge models the fleet node tag, the joined-without-the-required-tag state, and the refusal arm for a key/join tag mismatch -- including a node presenting no tags and sitting outside every grant. The breakage is a join performed outside that route; the read's identity axis checks the tag the node carries. The join itself stays with that module (and with the lane already handling butch)."),
      li("**Bring-up and enrollment order stays with gunbc.plans.new_server_bringup.**"),
    ]),
    h3("What this plan does not build on"),
    p("The report's swap item (3.3) is recorded as a mis-stated premise, not a defect. gunbc.host.memory_envelope::memory_envelope_verdict judges swap at the slot's cgroup (memory.swap.current, memory.swap.peak, memory.swap.max) and the user-work slice sets MemorySwapMax=0 per run; gunbc.host.host_swap_backing consults *host* swap only as backing for a cap that is declared nonzero, and the runner slot's declared memory_swap_max is zero -- so butch's 8 GiB /swap.img (priority -2) refuses nothing and blocks nothing. The axis (a presupposed limit resting on unobserved backing) stands, and host_swap_backing is its owner; that specimen does not. Any work item that cites 3.3 must cite this paragraph, not the swapfile."),
    h2("The instrument: one read, five comparisons"),
    p("Per DESIGN's Guarantees rule that an instrument is a row, not a flag, this instrument is stated with a consumer, a discriminator and a cost -- not as something a lane remembers to check."),
    ul([
      li("**Consumer (why it must exist, not merely run):** admission to the two rosters that already enumerate hosts. The build roster (gunbc.compute.remote_build_hosts::remote_build_serving_hosts feeding gunbc.compute.remote_build) and the serving roster (gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory with the router's replica layout). The consumer is already named in an operator decision: gunbc.design_ruling::butch_serving_retirement_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21 makes butch's admission as a remote-build host wait on a read of the host showing the serving units gone and its envelope measured. That is this instrument's consumer, stated before this plan existed. A second consumer is boot itself: a unit that would start against absent hardware should not start."),
      li("**Discriminator (what it proves, per axis):** a planted divergence must refuse, and the same shape in agreement must admit. Five pairs, one per axis -- a board removed from the model but enabled in a unit (HardwareGone); a unit enabled whose hardware does not enumerate (UnitOrphaned); a host whose enrolled fingerprint is not what the host answers with (IdentityUnanchored); a cap that binds a role below the residency that role declares (CapBelowResidency); a declared nonzero backing cap with no observed backing (BackingUnobserved). The arms must stay separate because their remedies are separate -- remove hardware, retire a unit, re-anchor a key, raise or refuse a cap, provision backing -- and one merged arm would put a host in permanent error while the operator waits for a sale or a reboot. gunbc.host.host_swap_backing's fail-closed fold is the pattern to copy, including the arm that refuses when nothing was read at all: never 'assume the default'."),
      li("**Cost:** one SSH round trip per host per pass, plus a bounded set of reads on it (a PCI class count, the enabled-unit list restricted to units the model declares, the host's key as the host presents it, the cgroup controls that bind each role, the swap total). It is not a new instrumentation surface: every read already exists as a converge step or an extdeps wire; the cost is the join and the cadence."),
      li("**Cadence, and who decided it:** the read rides the existing per-host fleet-converge route (gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_plan, gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_timer, gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow -- a mode of that workflow, which already carries observe-mode legs such as host_memory_observe) rather than a new scheduled job, because gunbc.design_ruling::job_roster_closed_to_growth closes the roster of scheduled jobs to growth without an operator decision. The operator ruled on 2026-11-21, in their words: 'A is fine' -- option A being that the read rides the existing per-host ctrl-fleet-converge timer as a new observe-only mode, no new job and no roster growth. That decision carries its gunbc.design_ruling row with the read's own change rather than on its own, because the ruling file sits inside both serving witness closures and a ruling-only merge would strand a lane mid-tally."),
    ]),
    h2("Done when"),
    ul([
      li("Each class the report names has exactly one row in gunbc.recurring_failure_mode, carrying a recognition rule, the rung it was found at, its ceiling and its next trigger, and no class is carried twice. Assessable against the tree by the roster, and the rows this plan names as its owners (fleet_hardware_fact_consumption, fleet_machine_authority, new_server_bringup, the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot) are cited, not duplicated."),
      li("A per-host read exists as a module with the five comparisons and the five typed divergence arms, and every arm's absence is distinguishable from the read not having happened."),
      li("Both rosters consume it: a host whose read is missing or divergent is refused admission and says which arm, rather than being admitted and degrading. Assessable by the roster fold's arms and the witness that plants each divergence."),
      li("The named orphan cases are closed by their owners' routes, not by hand: butch's strata-pool@a/@b under the retirement the operator performs personally (gunbc.design_ruling::butch_serving_retirement_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21) and dan's strata-expert-lock under the unit route, each followed by the read that shows the host agrees."),
      li("The enrolled-key roster is closed over the fleet, so a key mismatch means something: every host in both rosters carries an enrolled anchor (gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment covers srv1-srv4 and ken today), and no fleet route reaches a host by an address the model does not anchor."),
      li("The degradations the report measured are carried as figures in the rows that bear on them -- srv4 at 40 GiB against 56 GiB, the prefix-cache hit ratio under four concurrent agents against its declared checkpoint count -- so the next tuning decision reads a measurement instead of a default, and the capacity relation that already declares cache against slots (gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory::serving_warm_sessions_within) is joined to the concurrent-agent population rather than to a slot count."),
    ]),
  ]
}

data desired_state_follows_the_machine_plan: Plan = Plan {
  slug: "desired-state-follows-the-machine",
  title: "Desired state follows the machine: one per-host read of hardware, units, key identity, caps and backing, refused at roster admission where it diverges",
  body: desired_state_follows_the_machine_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen {
    condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Retire when a current per-host read of hardware, enabled units, host-key identity, binding caps and swap backing is what admits a host to the build and serving rosters (a missing read refusing, not an assumption), each of the five divergence arms has an owner and a planted-diversity witness, the enrolled-key roster is closed over both rosters, and no fleet lane discovers a drift by collision for a full quarter -- the collision discoveries being the only evidence that the read is not doing the work."),
  },
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly,
}
